    <section class="sec faq flush" id="faq">
      <div class="wrap faq-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="kicker reveal">Questions</div>
          <h2 class="h2 reveal d1">The fine print, <em>in plain words.</em></h2>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal d1">
          <details open>
            <summary>What do I need to generate an SDK?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              An OpenAPI spec and a <code>stainless.yml</code>.
              <code>ferrule generate --spec openapi.yml --config stainless.yml --out sdk</code> writes Python into an
              empty directory. <code>--target</code> selects go, java, kotlin, ruby, php or csharp. Until 0.1, run it
              from a checkout with <code>uv run ferrule …</code>, as the
              <a href={`${docs}quickstart/`}>quickstart</a> shows. The flags are the same.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Will my users have to change any code?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              On a new SDK, they import what you generated. Class and method names come from the config. If you are
              adopting a Stainless-shaped SDK, <code>ferrule verify</code> diffs the public API against the last
              release, and <code>adopt</code> runs that diff before anything is committed. The GitHub Action fails if a
              breaking change arrives without a label.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens to the code we wrote by hand?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              Adoption records every hand edit to a generated file as a patch under <code>.ferrule/patches/</code> and
              re-applies it three-way on each regeneration. Your <code>lib/</code> folder and <code>keep_files</code> are
              never touched. If a patch no longer applies, the run stops with <code>FER4102</code> and names the file.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is the generated SDK really ours?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              Yes. The SDK has no dependency on Ferrule or Forge, and the generated code carries no obligations. In
              adopt mode the runtime is copied from your own repository, so it stays under the licence you already
              ship.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is Ferrule affiliated with Stainless or Cloudflare?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              No. Ferrule is an independent Apache-2.0 project. It uses Cloudflare's open-source Forge engine as a
              pinned dependency, and treats public Stainless-generated SDKs (which are Apache-2.0) as the reference
              output.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How ready is it?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              Pre-alpha. All eight languages are on main. In Python, the OneBusAway SDK regenerates with zero breaking
              changes and all 1,110 of its own tests passing; the other languages' numbers are in the
              <a href="#languages">languages table</a>. It isn't on PyPI yet: the
              <a href={`${docs}quickstart/`}>quickstart</a> shows how to run it from source. Watch the fidelity board
              for your kind of SDK.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Only Python?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              No. Ferrule generates Python, TypeScript, Go, Java, Kotlin, Ruby, PHP and C#, all on main.
              <code>ferrule generate --target</code> selects Python (the default), Go, Java, Kotlin, Ruby, PHP and C#.
              Each language is checked against real public SDKs. The seven newer ones are generated from one
              language-neutral model, with one plugin per language; Python keeps its own adapter for now and moves onto
              that model later. CLI and Terraform have not started: no public generated CLI or provider pairs with a
              public config, so there is nothing to prove them against yet.
            </div>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What does it cost?<span class="pm"></span></summary>
            <div class="ans">
              Nothing. Everything that runs on your machine is open source. If there is ever a paid offering, it will be
              optional services like fixed-fee migrations with a signed fidelity report, never a token you need to
              generate code.
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

